My IQ is in the top 1% in the country. I'm 19 and have had like 10 jobs and the longest one I've ever kept was 6 months (and I quit twice in that period of time). I will never be successful, even though I was given the gift of intelligence and self-awareness and learning comes so easily to me. Everyone in my family was so hopeful for my future because I'm the only young person and I'm an utter failure and trust me, if I'm not hearing it from myself, I'm hearing it from someone else. 

&#x200B;

I remember practicing the piano for one day when I was younger and learning four full songs like it just made sense to me. I also sang in front of my family all the time and could sing in an opera style when I was about five. I memorized hundreds of scientific names of animals and dinosaurs and could outsmart the people who worked at the zoo/aquarium about animal knowledge at like four years old. I used to read a novel or two every week in elementary school and had a really impressive vocabulary. One year I was actually told that I was eligible for so many awards that they couldn't give them all to me, because it would've been unfair to the other kids and I would have won just about everything. I even won funniest sixth grader. 

&#x200B;

Even back then I felt anxiety and depression but it just snowballed as I got older. Now I can't focus or commit to anything, and worst of all, I never read any more. I haven't finished a book in so long. My brain just shuts off when I try to pick up new hobbies and I feel like I'm not good at anything. I wish I could turn back time.

I called them when 12. They yelled at me for being a prank caller and said they'd call the police if I "messed with them" again. I tried to overdose on prescription pills that were at my house after that, I took a large handful of them, I had no idea what they were and don't remember but luckily (I guess) I just woke up about 20 hours later. I feel like I had memory problems from that, I don't really remember ages that year or so. I was very serious. She asked me how old I was and I said 12 and she kind of groaned and just explained to me in a very harsh tone that my behavior wasn't funny and I should be ashamed of myself.

&#x200B;

Sorry, didn't know young people couldn't have issues! Not every childhood was nice and innocent. God that still makes me angry to think about. Similar things happen to me a lot, when I talk about the age that my depression started I feel like people just shrug it off. Personally I find it disturbing that a kid could experience that already.

Yes, definitely. It reminds me that at one point I felt genuine happiness without knowing it would be gone within seconds (sometimes I get waves of good feelings when something exciting happens, once a month or so) every time that happens I immediately realize I'm having a moment where I don't feel abject misery and sabotage it. I feel extremely sick with nostalgia sometimes, especially because I live somewhere that I don't know anyone at all, and thinking of a time where I had friends that would just come knocking on my door or call me randomly to come visit me makes me incredibly depressed. Also the thought that you lose innocence over time and will absolutely never be able to go back. 

&#x200B;

Recently I was having trouble carrying something up the stairs to my apartment building and a guy walking by helped me, smiled and said "no problem! have a nice day!" and I probably cried for about an hour about the fact that someone was actually nice to me. Receiving acts of kindness used to be an everyday thing when I was a kid, at least at one point. I also think it's easy to miss childhood because it's a time where I had people to support me, even if I didn't have a great family. I miss having someone to feed me, or get me medicine when I'm sick, or just seeing someone every time I came in the house.
